cat > virtq.py <<'N'
# SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-2.0-only
"""The device side of a split virtqueue.

desc:   list of dicts {"len": int, "flags": int, "next": int}
        flags: NEXT (1) means the chain continues at "next",
               WRITE (2) means the device writes into this buffer.
avail:  list (ring) of head indices; its length is the queue size.
avail_idx: free-running 16-bit counter of entries the driver has published.
last_avail: the counter value the device has consumed up to.

process() consumes entries last_avail .. avail_idx - 1 (mod 65536), the entry
for counter value n sits at avail[n % len(avail)].  For each head follow the
chain; "readable" is the total length of buffers without WRITE, "writable"
the total length of buffers with WRITE.  Call handler(readable, writable)
which returns how many bytes the device wants to write; the used length is
min(that, writable).  Append (head, used_len) to the used list.

A chain is malformed if "next" is outside the table or the chain has more
descriptors than the table has entries (a loop).  On a malformed chain stop at
once and report broken=True; entries already processed stay in `used`, and
last_avail is advanced only past the entries completed.
Return (used, last_avail, broken).
"""

NEXT = 1
WRITE = 2


def process(desc, avail, avail_idx, last_avail, handler):
    used = []
    # TODO
    return used, last_avail, False
N
